    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d782">

                <pb facs="1459" n="1425" sameAs="frus:frus1948v02/pg1425"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Control (Austria)/6–2348:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Legation in Austria </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-06-25T20:00:00-04:00">June 25, 1948—8 p.
                            m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>562. For Erhardt and Keyes. Circumstances of Marek’s arrest (urtels 787, June 19,
                    791 and 792, June 21, 802, June 22, and P–1998, June 21<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d782-f1"/>) and nature of Austrian protest on Migsch. case (P–2013,
                    June 23<anchor n="2" xml:id="d782-f2"/>) make necessary strongest support in AC
                    of Austrian position and condemnation of Soviet practices as contrary to all
                    existing international agreements. This support should take into account entire
                    general pattern of Soviet activities analyzed in your despatch 256, June
                        4<anchor n="2" xml:id="d782-f3"/> and center on unilateral threat to
                    Austrian Govt in recent Soviet arrests in order to prevent Soviet’s
                    concentrating on any particular ministry. In formulating policy in future, Dept
                    would appreciate clarification whether action taken in AC March 18 on notifying
                    Austrian Govt of arrests constitutes tacit recognition of Soviet right to make
                        arrests.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d782-f4"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Marshall</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d782-f1" n="1" xml:id="d782-f1-content">
                <p>None printed. Marek, a high-ranking Austrian political police official, was
                    arrested by Soviet authorities on June 17 because of alleged involvement in
                    anti-Soviet espionage activities.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d782-f2" n="2" xml:id="d782-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d782-f3" n="2" xml:id="d782-f3-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d782-f4" n="3" xml:id="d782-f4-content">
                <p>Legation Vienna replied in telegram 828, June 29, not printed (740.00119 Control
                    (Austria)/6–2948), that the right of any of the four powers to make arrests,
                    when the security of its forces was threatened, had never been questioned.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
